# Authorship

Prof. Shahab Anbarjafari <shb@3sholding.com> is the sole author, committer and
contributor of this repository. Every commit and every push must reflect that.

## Rules

- Commit as `Prof. Shahab Anbarjafari <shb@3sholding.com>`. The local repo config
  already sets `user.name` and `user.email`; never override them on a commit.
- Never add a `Co-Authored-By:` trailer, for any name, including AI assistants.
- Never add `Signed-off-by:`, `Generated with`, `Assisted-by:` or any similar
  attribution trailer.
- Never mention an AI tool, model or assistant in a commit message, tag message,
  release note or PR description.
- Author and committer must be identical. Do not use `--author` to split them.
- If a commit is ever created with the wrong identity, amend or rebase it so the
  history contains exactly one author and one committer.
- Naming a model or a tool as the *subject* of a change is fine, for example
  "fix the gpt-4o cost calculation". Naming one as the *author* of the change is not.

## Enforcement

`.githooks/commit-msg` **strips** an attribution trailer, because an editor or an agent
appends one automatically and refusing there would block the commit without removing the
trailer. It **refuses** a commit whose author or committer is anyone else, and one whose
message credits a tool in prose, since both of those need fixing at the source. It is
versioned, so it survives a fresh clone, but each clone has to point git at it once:

```sh
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
```

`scripts/check_authorship.py` checks the commits that actually exist rather than the
ones passing through a hook, so it catches anything added with `--no-verify`, in a clone
without `core.hooksPath` set, or by a rebase that replays a commit unchanged. CI runs it
on every push with full history.

```sh
python3 scripts/check_authorship.py
```

## Where the trailer comes from

Cursor appends `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` at the tooling level,
after the message is written and before the hook runs, so a rule cannot prevent it. Turn
it off at the source:

- **IDE agent**: Cursor Settings > Git & Pull Requests > Attribution (older versions:
  Agent > Attribution). Turn off commit attribution, and PR attribution too.
- **CLI agent**: `~/.cursor/cli-config.json` with
  `{"attribution": {"attributeCommitsToAgent": false, "attributePRsToAgent": false}}`.
- On an Enterprise plan, a team level setting can override both, and only an admin can
  change it.

The hook strips the trailer regardless, which is why it exists.

## Verification before pushing

```sh
git log --all --format='%an <%ae> | %cn <%ce>' | sort -u   # must print exactly one line
git log --all --format='%B' | grep -iE 'co-authored-by|signed-off-by|generated with'  # nothing
git for-each-ref refs/tags --format='%(taggername) %(taggeremail)' | sort -u  # one line
git config core.hooksPath   # must print .githooks
```
